  "worked_solution": "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nNote the following notation:\n\n- ${X}_{i}$ denotes the random variable representing the $i$th observation in a random sample, and\n\n- ${x}_{i}$ is the value of ${X}_{i}$ in a specific sample.\n\nTo find the median for the given sample, we arrange the data in numerical order:\n\n$$\n2.25,\\,2.65,\\,2.94,\\,3.06,\\,3.16\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{median}({x}_{1},…,{x}_{5})=2.94.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of our statistic for this particular sample is $2.94.$",
